<section class="panel" aria-label="Shared Architecture">
  <div class="panelInner">
    <h2 class="section-title">Shared Architecture</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      <b>Tools are the interface. The system is the operating model.</b>
      One set of signals, decision logic, and auditability runs end-to-end — so insights turn into action,
      decisions stay consistent, and automation stays safe and reviewable.
    </p>

    <div class="grid3" aria-label="Architecture blocks">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Signals</h3>
        <p class="muted">Named inputs that are comparable across workflows — so you can detect drift and measure impact.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Throughput, pass-through, and SLAs</li>
          <li>Open-text themes + reasons</li>
          <li>Capacity constraints (HM / recruiter / interview loops)</li>
          <li>Risk indicators + drift detection</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <h3>Decision Logic</h3>
        <p class="muted">Turn signals into "what should happen next" — with thresholds, tradeoffs, and escalation rules.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Expected ranges + alert thresholds</li>
          <li>Root-cause prompts ("why this surfaced")</li>
          <li>Recommended actions + impact tradeoffs</li>
          <li>Escalation rules as stakes increase</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <h3>Execution + Auditability</h3>
        <p class="muted">Execution happens inside the tools — and every action is reviewable.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Receipts attached to recommendations</li>
          <li>Approvals where needed (autonomy tiers)</li>
          <li>Action logs + change history</li>
          <li>"Why it did this" is visible, not implied</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" aria-label="How the system works">
  <div class="panelInner">
    <h2 class="section-title">How the system works</h2>
    <p class="muted">One operating model, viewed three ways: responsibilities, structure, and runtime behavior.</p>

    <div class="grid3" aria-label="How it works blocks">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>The layers</h3>
        <p class="muted">Separation of concerns so the org can scale without losing trust.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Systems environment</b> — ATS, HRIS, CRM, warehouse, comms.</li>
          <li><b>Signals</b> — normalized metrics + narrative inputs.</li>
          <li><b>Orchestration</b> — routing, SLAs, escalation, audit trails.</li>
          <li><b>Execution</b> — bounded actions + logged outcomes.</li>
          <li><b>Human judgment</b> — ethics, accountability, overrides.</li>
          <li><b>Core</b> — definitions, logic, thresholds, ownership.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <h3>System architecture</h3>
        <img
          src="/assets/diagrams/system.png"
          alt="AI-Native People Ops system diagram"
        />
        <p class="small">
          Human judgment stays at the top. Orchestration routes work.
          Signals and execution are distinct layers tied together by shared logic.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <h3>The operating loop</h3>
        <p class="muted">The minimum loop required for AI-native operations to work in production.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Signal</b> — collect events and outcomes.</li>
          <li><b>Interpret</b> — apply shared logic and thresholds.</li>
          <li><b>Route</b> — assign work or auto-execute at low risk.</li>
          <li><b>Act</b> — perform the action and log it.</li>
          <li><b>Learn</b> — feed outcomes back into the Core.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="small">Most AI pilots fail because they stop at <em>signal</em>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <div class="miniGrid" aria-label="Core/System/Tools legend">
      <div class="mini">
        <b>Core — Stable</b>
        <span>Source of truth: definitions, logic, thresholds, and ownership.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>System — Operational</b>
        <span>Governs how signals become decisions and actions, with guardrails and escalation.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Tools — Execute</b>
        <span>Interfaces and workflows that run inside the System and reference the Core.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      Tools reference the Core. They don't redefine it. The System keeps autonomy safe by making rules explicit and auditable.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" aria-label="What it replaces">
  <div class="panelInner">
    <h2 class="section-title">What it replaces</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      Most teams don't have a system — they have dashboards, spreadsheets, and one-off automations.
      That creates three predictable failures:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Truth drift:</strong> definitions change silently and metrics stop matching across tools.</li>
      <li><strong>Decision drift:</strong> rules are implicit ("it depends"), so outcomes vary by person and mood.</li>
      <li><strong>Execution drift:</strong> actions aren't routed, tracked, or closed — so work repeats and trust erodes.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout">
      The goal isn't more analytics. It's a governed path from signal → decision → action → learning.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" aria-label="Design principles">
  <div class="panelInner">
    <h2 class="section-title">Design principles</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>AI is infrastructure</strong> — embedded where teams already work.</li>
      <li><strong>Humans remain accountable</strong> — escalation paths and auditability are built in.</li>
      <li><strong>Governance is continuous</strong> — rules evolve with the business, not quarterly deck cycles.</li>
      <li><strong>The system is operable</strong> — designed to run under real-world constraints.</li>
    </ul>

